  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nModel trends aren't looking great to kick off September, as hot and \ndry conditions are very likely to prevail. Both deterministic and \nensemble datasets depict high pressure being nudged eastward over \nthe southeast CONUS through the week, as persistent troughing \nsettles atop the Pacific northwest. Although this does loosen the \ngrip of ridging over the Panhandles, it should still have enough \ninfluence to reduce moisture transport to the CWA while keeping high \ntemperatures in the 90 to 105 range each day this week. If there are \nany days with better rain chances, models suggest Tue could see \nslightly improved potential as the jet stream sags closer to the \nPanhandles. However, this still only supports meager 10-20% POPs \nto far northwestern portions of the forecast area. Elevated fire \nweather conditions may return any given day with breezy winds, but\nwe'll monitor trends for later this week to see if troughing \ncould provide better rain opportunities again. \n\nHarrel",
